Z||000000||WisconsinGT||04-05-2000||08:46 PM||||a manual or a GM parts book for me? I was at the local GM dealer looking for gaskets. Here is the problem...If you have an 88GT look at your EGR Valve. Then look below the adaptor. You should see the adaptor. Below the adaptor you should see a flat metal spacer with a gasket above and below it. This is what I have. Anyway, the GM dealers computer did not show the flat metal piece. So I need to know from others if the flat metal piece is normal. Thanks.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Formula||04-05-2000||09:22 PM||||could I look at my 88 Formula too?  <P>mine doesnt have a flat metal piece between the valve and the adapter.||||reg||1
Z||000002||80's BOY||04-05-2000||10:10 PM||||it's a gimmick!! put it back together and go with it, man. if you don't hear or see blowby you'll do just fine!!!||||reg||1
Z||000003||88 Database Nut||04-06-2000||07:47 AM||||Hey Whisky, here's the info from the 22P book, page 3-45 in the green section.<BR>From the bottom towards the top, there is the following:<P>10038299   GASKET, EGR VLV TO EXH CR/OVR<BR>10034074   ADAPTER, EGR VLV<BR>10038298   GASKET, EGR VLV TO ADAP<BR>17110813   VALVE, EGR<P>This is for 1985-1988 V6s.  There may be variations to the EGR number, and ALL of these have no doubt changed, but this should get you started.  For the parts guys at the dealer, this is in group/section 3.671<P>I am not sure if I understand your info about the metal spacer, I don't have them on my 88GTs.  I would think that this would push the EGR TUBE too far up against the throttle body coolant tubes.<P>Good Luck||||reg||12
Z||000004||wkayl||04-06-2000||09:40 AM||||Here it is an 86GT.  No extra plate here.<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/egr_1.jpg">  <BR>  <P>[This message has been edited by wkayl (edited 04-06-2000).]<p>[This message has been edited by wkayl (edited 04-06-2000).]||||reg||1
